\paragraph{Quantization.}

To store or transmit a sampled signal digitally, one quantizes its values to finite precision.

Section~\ref{sec-transform-coding} develops transform coding, in which an invertible linear transform, usually orthogonal, is applied before quantization. Transforming the data can make the coefficients easier to compress. Directly quantizing the sampled values is a simpler alternative.

For example, a step size $s=1/N$ gives the finite vector $(u_n \eqdef f(n/N))_{n=1}^{N} \in \RR^N$. This records only finitely many samples, which do not determine a general bandlimited signal. Exact recovery from these data requires additional assumptions, such as a specified finite-dimensional signal model.

\begin{figure}[tbp]
\centering
\BookDrawing{tikz/shannon-quantizer}
\caption{A uniform scalar quantizer.}
\label{fig-review-shannon-quantizer}
\end{figure}

For a quantization step $T>0$, the quantizer $v_n = Q_T(u_n) \in \ZZ$ records the index of the nearest multiple of $T$, i.e.
\eq{
	v = Q_T(u) \quad\Leftrightarrow\quad
	v-\frac{1}{2} \leq u/T < v+\frac{1}{2},
}
as illustrated by the quantizer diagram. Dequantization replaces each index by the cell midpoint $D_T(v) \eqdef T v$, which minimizes the worst-case error within that cell. The resulting error is bounded by $T/2$: $|D_T(Q_T(u))-u| \leq T/2$.

