\begin{figure}[tbp]
\centering
\BookDrawing{tikz/ml-pca-variance}
\caption{Principal directions of variation.}
\label{fig-review-ml-pca-variance}
\end{figure}
The PCA basis consists of the right singular vectors of the centered data matrix. Proposition~\ref{prop-svd} gives the reduced singular value decomposition
\eq{
 	\tilde X = \sqrt{n} U \diag(\si) V^*
}
where $U \in \RR^{n \times r}$ and $V \in \RR^{p \times r}$ have orthonormal columns, and $r=\rank(\tilde X) \leq \min(n,p)$.

Write $V=(v_k)_{k=1}^r$ for the orthonormal columns, which are eigenvectors of $\hat C = V \diag(\si^2) V^\top$, with $v_k \in \RR^p$. These vectors describe the principal directions of variation in the point cloud $(x_i)_i$ in $\RR^p$.

We order the singular values as $\si_1 \geq \ldots \geq \si_r$, so the leading components account for the greatest variance.

Figure~\ref{fig-cov} displays an empirical covariance matrix and its spectrum $(\si_k^2)_k$ for the Iris dataset\footnote{\url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iris_flower_data_set}} introduced by Fisher. It contains 50 samples from each of three iris species: Iris setosa, Iris virginica, and Iris versicolor. The four features are sepal length, sepal width, petal length, and petal width.

To obtain a PCA embedding $x_i \in \RR^p \mapsto z_i \in \RR^d$ of dimension $d \leq p$, project onto the first $d$ right singular vectors, extending the basis if $d>r$:
\eql{\label{eq-pca-1}
	z_i \eqdef ( \dotp{x_i-\hat m}{v_k} )_{k=1}^d \in \RR^d.
}
The corresponding reconstruction is
\eql{\label{eq-pca-2}
	\tilde x_i\eqdef\hat m+\sum_{k=1}^d z_{i,k}v_k \in \RR^p.
}
Thus $\tilde x_i = \Proj_{\tilde T}(x_i)$, where $\tilde T\eqdef\hat m+ \Span_{k=1}^d(v_k)$ is the affine reconstruction space.

Figure~\ref{fig-pca} shows an example of PCA for 2-D and 3-D visualization.

