\section{Supervised Learning: Classification}
\label{sec-classif}

For classification, the labels are discrete: $y_i \in \Yy = \{1,\ldots,k\}$.

We study two standard methods: nearest neighbors and logistic classification.

Both methods provide useful baselines for assessing more elaborate models.

Nearest-neighbor methods also apply to regression.


\subsection{Nearest-Neighbor Classification}
\label{sec-nn-classif}

The $R$-nearest-neighbor method ($R$-NN) classifies an input using the labels of its $R$ nearest training observations. Increasing $R$ reduces sensitivity to individual noisy labels, but excessive smoothing can obscure class boundaries. For consistency, a standard asymptotic choice has $R=R_n\to\infty$ and $R_n/n\to0$.

Nearest-neighbor classification is a useful baseline, especially when the feature dimension $p$ is small.

The prediction $\hat f(x)\in\Yy$ is the most frequent label among those $R$ observations, with a fixed rule for breaking ties. The method is nonparametric: its representation depends on the training sample rather than on a fixed-dimensional parameter vector.

\begin{figure}[tbp]
\centering
\BookDrawing{tikz/ml-nearest-neighbors}
\caption{Nearest neighbors.}
\label{fig-review-ml-nearest-neighbors}
\end{figure}
First compute the Euclidean distance from the query $x$ to each training observation $x_i$.

Sorting the distances generates an indexing $\si$ (a permutation of $\{1,\ldots,n\}$) such that 
\eq{
 	\norm{x-x_{\si(1)}} \leq \norm{x-x_{\si(2)}} \leq \ldots \leq \norm{x-x_{\si(n)}}. 
}
For a given $R$, one can compute the ``local'' histogram of classes around $x$
\eq{
	h_\ell(x)\eqdef\frac1R\#\{i\in\{1,\ldots,R\}:y_{\si(i)}=\ell\}.
}
The predicted class at $x$ is an index attaining the largest histogram value:
\eq{
	\hat f(x) \in \uargmax{\ell} h_\ell(x).
}

Choose $R$ by validation or cross-validation. For classification, the validation risk~\eqref{eq-valid-risk} typically uses the 0--1 loss, giving the fraction of misclassified observations
\eq{
	R_{\bar n}\eqdef\frac1{\bar n}\sum_{j=1}^{\bar n}\de(\bar y_j-\hat f(\bar x_j))
}
where $\de(0)=0$ and $\de(s)=1$ if $s \neq 0$. 

The method also applies to features in a general metric space in place of $\RR^p$.

Fast nearest-neighbor search can avoid explicitly sorting every distance.

