\subsection{Multiclass Logistic Classification}
\label{sec-multiclass-logit}

For multiclass logistic classification, introduce one weight vector for each of the
$k$ classes. Collect the vectors $\be = ( \be_\ell )_{\ell=1}^k$ as columns of $\be \in \RR^{p \times k}$.

For an input $x \in \RR^p$, convert the class scores into probabilities
using the softmax model:
\eql{\label{eq-multiclass-logitmap}
	 f(x,\be) = \pa{ \frac{ e^{\dotp{x}{\be_\ell}} }{ \sum_m e^{\dotp{x}{\be_m}} } }_\ell
}
The vector $f(x,\be)\in[0,1]^k$ gives the probabilities that $x$ belongs
to each class, and satisfies $\sum_\ell f(x,\be)_\ell=1$.

Estimate $\be$ by maximizing the log-likelihood
over the training sample:
 \eq{
	 \umax{\be \in \RR^{p \times k}} \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \log( f(x_i,\be)_{y_i} ) 
}
where $y_i \in \Yy = \{1,\ldots,k\}$ is the label of
observation $x_i$.

This is conveniently rewritten as
\eq{
	 \umin{\be \in \RR^{p \times k}} \Ee(\be)\eqdef\frac1n\left(\sum_i\text{LSE}(X\be)_i-\dotp{X\be}{D}\right)
}
where $D \in \{0,1\}^{n \times k}$ is the one-hot class matrix
\eq{
	  D_{i,\ell} = \choice{
          1 \qifq y_i=\ell, \\
          0 \text{otherwise}.
      }
}
and LSE is the log-sum-exp operator
\eq{
	 \text{LSE}(S)_i=\log\left(\sum_\ell\exp(S_{i,\ell})\right),\qquad\text{LSE}(S)\in\RR^n.
}
For $k=2$, identify the first class with label $+1$ and the second with label $-1$. The model then agrees with binary logistic classification in Section~\ref{sec-two-class-logit}, with parameter $\be_1-\be_2$. A single weight vector therefore suffices in that case.

Direct evaluation of log-sum-exp can
overflow when $S_{i,\ell}$ is large. A stable evaluation
subtracts the largest entry in each row before exponentiation,
using the identity
\eq{
	\text{LSE}(S+a)=\text{LSE}(S)+a
} 
for a rowwise constant $a$. This shift is especially useful for well-separated classes, whose parameter vectors $\be_\ell$ may grow large.


The gradient of the sum of the rowwise log-sum-exp values is the softmax operator
\eq{
	  \nabla_S\sum_i\text{LSE}(S)_i = \text{SM}(S) \eqdef
      \pa{
          \frac{
                  e^{S_{i,\ell}}
              }{
                  \sum_m e^{S_{i,m}}
              } }   
}
As with log-sum-exp, subtract the largest entry in each row before evaluating the exponentials.

